# `Compose batch body` — worked example

Uses your real site, list GUID and entity type. The only literals are those three plus the
boundary names; everything else comes from the decision objects the script returned.

**Why two Selects instead of one with an `if()`:** a create and an update differ by two header
lines. Expressing that as a conditional inside the template means building the newlines with
`decodeUriComponent('%0D%0A')`, because Power Automate won't accept a literal line break inside
an `if()`. Two fixed templates keep every newline literal, and when a batch 400s you can read
the template and see exactly what it emits.

Actions, in order, inside `Do until — batches`:

---

## 1. `Compose batch slice`

```
take(skip(body('Parse_result')?['decisions'], variables('varBatchOffset')), 100)
```

## 2. `Filter updates` — Data Operation → Filter array

**From:** `outputs('Compose_batch_slice')`
**Condition:** `item()?['action']` **is equal to** `update`

## 3. `Filter creates` — Data Operation → Filter array

**From:** `outputs('Compose_batch_slice')`
**Condition:** `item()?['action']` **is equal to** `create`

---

## 4. `Select update parts`

**From:** `body('Filter_updates')`
Switch the map to **text mode** (the icon at the right of the Map box) and paste this as the
single value. Line breaks are literal — type them, don't encode them.

```
--batch_@{variables('varRunId')}
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=changeset_@{variables('varRunId')}_@{item()?['excelRow']}

--changeset_@{variables('varRunId')}_@{item()?['excelRow']}
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

POST https://deutschebank.sharepoint.com/sites/simri/_api/web/lists(guid'6b659861-abd0-4e45-b74e-63e3f69f2648')/items(@{item()?['spId']}) HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose
IF-MATCH: *
X-HTTP-Method: MERGE

@{concat('{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},', substring(string(item()?['fields']), 1))}

--changeset_@{variables('varRunId')}_@{item()?['excelRow']}--
```

## 5. `Select create parts`

**From:** `body('Filter_creates')`

Identical except: no `(@{item()?['spId']})` on the URL, and no `IF-MATCH` / `X-HTTP-Method`
lines.

```
--batch_@{variables('varRunId')}
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=changeset_@{variables('varRunId')}_@{item()?['excelRow']}

--changeset_@{variables('varRunId')}_@{item()?['excelRow']}
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

POST https://deutschebank.sharepoint.com/sites/simri/_api/web/lists(guid'6b659861-abd0-4e45-b74e-63e3f69f2648')/items HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose

@{concat('{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},', substring(string(item()?['fields']), 1))}

--changeset_@{variables('varRunId')}_@{item()?['excelRow']}--
```

### The `fields` expression

```
concat('{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},', substring(string(item()?['fields']), 1))
```

`string(item()?['fields'])` gives `{"FirstName":"Ana","LastName":"Popescu",...}`.
`substring(..., 1)` drops the leading `{`. Prepending the `__metadata` block produces a valid
body without listing 20 fields by hand.

⚠️ **The keys are still Excel header names.** SharePoint needs internal names, so `Legal entity`
must become `Legal_x0020_entity`. Until the script emits internal names directly, this
expression sends the wrong keys and SharePoint will reject the unknown ones. Send me the
`fields?$select=Title,InternalName` output and I'll add the mapping to the script.

---

## 6. `Compose batch body`

```
concat(join(body('Select_update_parts'), ''), join(body('Select_create_parts'), ''), '--batch_', variables('varRunId'), '--')
```

`join` with an empty separator, because each part already ends with a newline. The trailing
`--batch_<runId>--` closes the batch and is what SharePoint looks for to know the payload is
complete — omit it and you get a 400 that says nothing useful.

## 7. `Send batch`

SharePoint → Send an HTTP request to SharePoint

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Site Address | `https://deutschebank.sharepoint.com/sites/simri` |
| Method | `POST` |
| Uri | `_api/$batch` |
| Headers | `Content-Type` : `multipart/mixed; boundary=batch_@{variables('varRunId')}` |
| Body | `outputs('Compose_batch_body')` |

The action's own Uri is **relative**. The `POST` lines *inside* the body must be **absolute**.
Getting those the wrong way round is the most common 400 here.

---

## What it actually renders

One update (Excel row 2 → item 1042) and one create (Excel row 4), with
`varRunId` = `a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714`:

```
--batch_a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=changeset_a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714_2

--changeset_a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714_2
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

POST https://deutschebank.sharepoint.com/sites/simri/_api/web/lists(guid'6b659861-abd0-4e45-b74e-63e3f69f2648')/items(1042) HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose
IF-MATCH: *
X-HTTP-Method: MERGE

{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},"FirstName":"Ana","LastName":"Popescu","PhoneNr":"+40712345678","ICC_ID":"8935201234567890125","Status":"Active"}

--changeset_a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714_2--
--batch_a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=changeset_a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714_4

--changeset_a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714_4
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

POST https://deutschebank.sharepoint.com/sites/simri/_api/web/lists(guid'6b659861-abd0-4e45-b74e-63e3f69f2648')/items HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose

{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},"FirstName":"Elena","LastName":"Dumitru","PhoneNr":"+40745998877","ICC_ID":"8935201234567890992","Status":"Active"}

--changeset_a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714_4--
--batch_a7f3c9e1-4b2d-4f8a-9c31-6de0f2a5b714--
```

Note the changeset suffixes `_2` and `_4` — those are the Excel row numbers. When you parse the
response, they tell you which spreadsheet row each result belongs to. Without them you'd be
matching responses to requests by position and hoping nothing reordered.

---

## Formatting rules that will silently 400 you

- **Blank line after every header block.** One after `Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary`, one after the last request header, before the JSON body. Miss one and the parser reads your JSON as a header.
- **No blank line between the boundary and the `Content-Type` that follows it.**
- **CRLF line endings.** Power Automate's text-mode box produces these; hand-assembling with `\n` alone does not.
- **Each changeset closes with `--`** (`--changeset_..._2--`) and so does the batch.
- **Nothing after the final `--batch_<id>--`.** A trailing newline is tolerated; anything else isn't.

## Test it with three rows

Make the middle row deliberately invalid — a `Status` value the column rejects is easiest. Then
confirm rows 1 and 3 still committed. If all three rolled back, your changesets aren't isolating
and the per-row reporting the whole design rests on won't work.

Do this before wiring up the response parser. Ten minutes now, versus discovering it at 60,000
rows.
